first let's just try and clear this up  what's your advice to the public today  if they need to call 999 I want an  ambulance sent well it's to use their  Common Sense the the system will be  under very severe pressure today the  trade unions have refused to give  National exemptions in terms of all  life-threatening and emergency calls  they've said that they will prioritize  those but it'll be down to local  agreements with trust and indeed some  decisions taken in call centers so the  system will be under significant  pressure and therefore we were saying to  the public to exercise their common  sense in terms of what activities they  do be mindful of the fact that there are  those pressures on the system and should  local decisions are they not the best  ones to take rather than having a  national plan is that one of your  concerns there is no National  contingency plan in place is that your  job well the difficulty of putting  contingency measures in place is given  the uncertainty as to what exactly is  and is not being covered and the fact  that those decisions in some cases will  be taken on the day but it's also the  case that even what's referred to as  Category 3  urgent calls of often very serious as  well so we're doing all we can with NHS  colleagues so put in place contingency  measures we've got the Army we've got  volunteers we've got community services  we've got frail elderly services in  terms of people that might fall so  there's a range of measures from a  contingency perspective that we've been  putting in place but there's obviously  limits to what that can be done and  therefore it is down to the trade unions  in terms of what exemptions they provide  and the concern is particularly if  services are taken from the picket line  that adds further confusion on the day  and potentially delays responses as I  could State you said in your piece this  morning in the the telegraph that the  only way to resolve this is by talking  the unions wants to talk to you they  keep saying that but they want to talk  to you about pay so why wouldn't you do  that well we have a process in terms of  pay an independent process and we've  accepted the recommendations in full and  that looks at both the pressures on the  NHS and indeed at the Autumn statement  additional funding was targeted to the  NHS the chancellor allocated an extra  6.6 billion pounds to the NHS but he  also put in place the biggest ever  increase in Social care funding an extra  7.5 billion pounds over the next two  years and that recognizes particularly  for paramedics one of the biggest issues  that raised with me when I was talking  to ambulance staff on Sunday evening  actually what they said to me was more  important than pay was the frustration  around Handover delays at hospitals and  they're often caused by the delay in  getting patients out of Hospital who are  fit to be discharged that's why the  chancellor invested the extra 7.5  billion pounds in Social care an extra  two hundred thousand social care  packages so we're investing in the NHS  we're investing in uh social care and I  don't want to divert money from those  essential Services focused on patients  uh to overturn what has been an  independent process which has looked at  what is Affordable to the economy what  is Affordable to your viewers at a time  or cost of living pressure but also  recognizing the systems under very  severe pressure and we need to get that  extra investment into the NHS and into  social care uh we'll come back to some  of the points you make there in a moment  section of state but I just want to read  you um what's been said by the NHS  providers and NHS Confederation  yesterday they said that the fear of NHS  leaders is that the risk to patients is  only going to get worse with future  strikes plans that is unless the  government is able to reach agreement  with the trade unions to bring a swift  end to the dispute now that's not the  union saying that that's not nurses  saying that that is the NHS providers  and this is on the government on you to  come to an agreement well we have as I  say accepted in full the pay-per-view  body recommendations but that's in  addition to last year let me just let me  just interrupt you there sir I'm sorry  to do that but you always talk about the  pay review bodies and you know as well  as I do that the pay review body is not  always listened to back in 2014 the pay  review body recommended a one percent  increase in NHS pay that was rejected by  the now Chancellor Jeremy Hunt in 2018  police officers were recommended a pay  review increase of two percent that was  rejected as well so you can make a  decision to not listen into the  pay-per-view body why are you not doing  it in this instance and when we have  done that you you have been the first to  I'm sure to to criticize us for not  respecting the independence the  difference here is we have accepted  those recommendations in full because we  recognize the system coming out of the  pandemic has been under pressure so we  have accepted the recommendations in  full and that comes on top of last year  when the rest of the public sector had a  pay freeze we prioritized the NHS with  an extra three percent rise when many  other people were facing a pay free so  we have prioritized uh the NHS we've  accepted in full the independent pay  review bodies recommendations but we've  gone further and we've put an extra 6.6  billion pounds into the NHS and the  record funding into social care because  we recognize the shoe strains the NHS is  in as a consequence of the pandemic and  it's important that we focus on those  patient services to clear the the  backlogs in operations to address the  Handover delays to get this support into  primary care for GPS because that's  where the system is under most pressure  let me put let me put this to Mr Barkley  without having staff in the Health  Service because they're not being paid  they're not working in the right  conditions you won't be able to improve  those services and treatment outcomes  well it pay pay is a flat too I mean the  basic pay of a paramedic the average pay  of a paramedic is 34 300 but when you  add in overtime non-social ads that's an  extra 37 takes it up to 47  000 on top of that you get a 20 a fifth  going into pension as well so we have  listened to the recommendations of their  pay body on pay but pay is just one of a  number of factors we also look at the  fleet we're investing in more ambulances  we're putting more money into ambulance  trusts and extra 150 million pounds  we're also investing in call centers  because we've seen very significant  increases in pressure on the number of  calls that ambulance trusts are facing  so we put an extra 50 million pounds  into that and as well as that we  recognize there's a particular issue in  terms of the elderly those elderly uh  people that fall the need to get  services to them that's why we've been  working with Partners like Saint John's  ambulance an extra 30 million in to get  Saint John's ambulance targeting those  elderly fools so we're investing more in  the system we're doing that within the  context of an independent process that  looks at pay but it's also important  that we tackle some of these system  pressures particularly around social  care that's why we put the record  funding into that and that in turn has a  very big impact on paramedics that is  going to take a while to feed through as  you well know now you mentioned in in  the telegraph today that uh striking  workers are taking a conscious decision  to put patients at risk that's not  conciliatory language that's not going  to bring the two sides closers together  that's only going to harden uh the side  against you isn't it well it's just a  reflection of fact the trade unions have  chosen this time for the strike we saw  over the last week a 73 increase in flu  admissions to hospital we saw a 27  increase in covid emissions we've got  five times the normal prescribing rate  of prescriptions for antibiotics in  terms of strep a we've had a massive  increasing calls to 111 up by more than  a third we've got an increase in calls  to 999 over the last month of 15 so the  system is facing very significant  pressures as a result of flu of covid of  strep a and it's at this time that trade  unionism not only chosen to strike but  have refused to put in place National  exemptions to say they will cover all of  the life-threatening and emergency cat  one cat two calls that in turn makes the  contingency measures much more difficult  to play and I'm sure the Unions would  say that they're not putting patients at  risk you're actually putting patients at  risk by not willing not being willing to  discuss their pay requests now this has  been sold in Scotland by meeting the pay  requests of uh striking staff there  would the government go to something  like that seven and a half percent for  nurses to try and resolve these strikes  one of the big misconceptions about the  the deal in Scotland and incidentally  the GMB were opposed to the deal in  Scotland they're one of the trade unions  that are striking but it wasn't simply  seven and a half percent if you add in  all the other measures in terms of  reducing hours extra annual leave  changes to the way over time is address  protected learning time actually the  cost of that is much more significant  and as I say one of the trade unions has  rejected that as well but what we want  to do is focus on patience get that  investment into the NHS into social Care  address the blockage there are patients  that are ready to leave Hospital who is  not in their interest to stay so get  that social care and flow working that  is what we prioritized and we've done  that whilst also listening to what is an  independent process around the  pay-per-view bodies we've accepted those  recommendations in full and that builds  on the extra three percent we awarded  last year at a time when other public  sector workers face to freeze  okay Steve Barkley uh thanks for being  with us

